--TEST--
`Core\Validate::isMac` is one predicate over a value rather than three predicates over three notations: every spelling of the same twelve characters is counted, and the count is unanimous for all 137 values swept — docs/spec/01-core-library.md § 12
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 12 accepts a MAC three ways — six octets by `:`, six by `-`, or Cisco's
// three groups of four by `.` — in hex digits of either case. The sibling case
// `validate-mac-takes-three-spellings-and-refuses-the-nearest-miss.nvst` names
// each of those bounds beside the spelling just past it. This one asserts what
// those rows are examples *of*: the notation is not part of the question. A
// value is twelve characters; the separator and the case decide only how it is
// written down, so all nine spellings of one value must agree, and a member
// that grew its own reading of one notation fails on the count here while
// still answering plausibly on every named line over there.
//
// The invariant is counted rather than read off: for each value, how many of
// its nine spellings are accepted. Only `0` and `9` are agreements, so a single
// spelling that parted company from its eight siblings shows up as a value
// missing from the unanimity count — without this case having to know which
// spelling it was. `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/validate.rs`'s `# isMac` section owns
// the line itself, and records that it has no divergence from PHP to state.
final class Mac {
    // The value in chunks of `$width`, joined by `$sep`. Written as a loop over
    // the value's own length so that a value of the wrong length renders in the
    // same three notations as a well-formed one — a short last chunk is exactly
    // what the member is being asked about.
    public static function render(string $v, string $sep, int $width): string {
        string $out = "";
        int $len = Core\Str::length($v) as int;
        int $i = 0;
        for (; $i < $len; $i = $i + $width) {
            int $take = $width;
            if ($i + $take > $len) {
                $take = $len - $i;
            }
            if ($i > 0) {
                $out = $out . $sep;
            }
            $out = $out . Core\Str::slice($v, $i, $take);
        }
        return $out;
    }

    public static function hit(string $s): int {
        return Core\Validate::isMac($s) ? 1 : 0;
    }

    // The three separator forms of one already-cased value.
    public static function forms(string $v): int {
        return Mac::hit(Mac::render($v, ":", 2))
             + Mac::hit(Mac::render($v, "-", 2))
             + Mac::hit(Mac::render($v, ".", 4));
    }

    // All nine spellings: three separators, each of the value as written, lower
    // cased and upper cased. Hex case is no bound at all, so it belongs on this
    // side of the count rather than in a row of its own.
    public static function accepts(string $v): int {
        return Mac::forms($v)
             + Mac::forms(Core\Str::lower($v))
             + Mac::forms(Core\Str::upper($v));
    }
}

// What the renderer actually produces, for the one value everybody writes. A
// unanimity count is satisfied by a member that refuses everything, so the
// counts below mean nothing unless these three are the addresses § 12 names.
echo "renders ",
     Mac::render("001122334455", ":", 2), " ",
     Mac::render("001122334455", "-", 2), " ",
     Mac::render("001122334455", ".", 4),
     "\n";

// The content sweep: every code point below 128 in the last character's place,
// each value spelled all nine ways. Unanimity must hold for all 128, and the
// 22 accepted ones are `0`-`9`, `A`-`F` and `a`-`f` — the same 22 the sibling
// case finds through the colon form alone, here found through three notations
// at once, which is why the answer count is nine times it. The three separators
// are themselves in this range: a `:` in the last place makes the hyphen and
// dot renderings contain a colon too, and all nine still agree.
int $unanimous = 0;
int $accepted = 0;
int $answers = 0;
int $c = 0;
for (; $c < 128; $c = $c + 1) {
    int $n = Mac::accepts("00112233445" . Core\Str::fromCodePoint($c as uint));
    if ($n == 0 || $n == 9) {
        $unanimous = $unanimous + 1;
    }
    if ($n == 9) {
        $accepted = $accepted + 1;
    }
    $answers = $answers + $n;
}
echo "sweep-unanimous ", $unanimous as string, "/128\n";
echo "sweep-accepted ", $accepted as string, "\n";
echo "sweep-answers ", $answers as string, "\n";

// The same invariance over the shapes a sweep of one position cannot reach:
// four well-formed values differing only in hex case, a value too short and one
// too long, a non-hex digit, an embedded space, and the empty string. Length is
// where the three notations could most plausibly disagree — ten characters is
// five octets or two-and-a-half Cisco groups — and they do not.
array<string> $values = [
    "001122334455",
    "aabbccddeeff",
    "AABBCCDDEEFF",
    "aAbBcCdDeEfF",
    "0011223344",
    "00112233445566",
    "00112233445g",
    "0011223344 5",
    "",
];
int $tu = 0;
int $ta = 0;
int $tn = 0;
int $i = 0;
for (; $i < 9; $i = $i + 1) {
    int $m = Mac::accepts($values[$i as string]);
    if ($m == 0 || $m == 9) {
        $tu = $tu + 1;
    }
    if ($m == 9) {
        $ta = $ta + 1;
    }
    $tn = $tn + $m;
}
echo "table-unanimous ", $tu as string, "/9\n";
echo "table-accepted ", $ta as string, "\n";
echo "table-answers ", $tn as string, "\n";
--EXPECT--
renders 00:11:22:33:44:55 00-11-22-33-44-55 0011.2233.4455
sweep-unanimous 128/128
sweep-accepted 22
sweep-answers 198
table-unanimous 9/9
table-accepted 4
table-answers 36
